+<main><div class="paper">
+<header class="cover"><div class="kicker">Production Rework</div><h1>DataRework User Manual</h1>
+<p class="lead">How to use every function of DataRework.exe — signing in, searching records, correcting data, managing containers and reporting damage.</p>
+<p class="meta">Edition: English · Matches the application as of commit 56d0739 · 2026-10-08<br>
+Screenshots use sample data, not real production records.</p></header>
+<section class="chapter" id="about"><h2><span class="num">1.</span>What DataRework does</h2>
+<p>DataRework (the window is titled <b>Production Rework</b>) is the desktop tool used to correct production packaging records after the fact. Every finished product <b>unit</b> is recorded together with the serial numbers of the <b>display box</b>, <b>inner box</b> and <b>carton</b> it was packed into. When a label was scanned wrongly, a unit sits in the wrong box, or a box is damaged, an authorised operator uses DataRework to find the records, correct them, and keep the serial inventory consistent.</p>
+
+<h3>How products are packed</h3>
+<div class="chain">
+  <span class="lvl carton">Carton</span><span class="arrow">contains →</span>
+  <span class="lvl inner">Inner</span><span class="arrow">contains →</span>
+  <span class="lvl display">Display</span><span class="arrow">contains →</span>
+  <span class="lvl unit">Unit</span>
+</div>
+<p>The first three levels are called <b>containers</b> because they hold other things. A unit is the smallest item and holds nothing.</p>
+
+<h3>Words used in this manual</h3>
+<table class=""><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Serial / Serial No</b></td><td>The unique code printed on a label. Units, displays, inners and cartons each have one.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Roll No</b></td><td>The label roll a serial was printed from. It follows the serial automatically when a serial is changed.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Record (row)</b></td><td>One saved line for one unit, carrying the serials of every container it sits in.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Link</b></td><td>The saved connection between an item and the container directly above it.</td></tr><tr><td><b>In use / Free</b></td><td>Every serial in the serial inventory is either <i>in use</i> (attached to something in production) or <i>free</i>. Fixing records moves serials between the two states.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Damaged</b></td><td>A free serial that has been flagged as unusable (see Report damage).</td></tr></tbody></table>
+</section><section class="chapter pb" id="start"><h2><span class="num">2.</span>Before you start</h2>
+<h3>The program files</h3>
+<p>DataRework is delivered as a folder. Keep these three files together; copy the whole folder when moving to another computer.</p>
+<table class=""><thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>DataRework.exe</code></td><td>The application you open and work in.</td></tr><tr><td><code>SerialStoreSync.exe</code></td><td>A background program that keeps the serial usage counts up to date (see below). You never open it yourself.</td></tr><tr><td><code>nssm.exe</code></td><td>The helper that installs the background program as a Windows service.</td></tr></tbody></table>
+
+<h3>The background sync service</h3>
+<p>The first time DataRework opens on a computer, Windows shows a <b>User Account Control (UAC)</b> prompt. It is asking permission to install the <b>DataRework Serial Store Sync</b> service. Choose <b>Yes</b> (an administrator password may be requested).</p>
+<ul><li>The service starts automatically with Windows and keeps running after you close DataRework.</li><li>About every <b>10 seconds</b> it recounts how many serials are <i>in use</i> and <i>damaged</i> for each roll. After you save a change, those totals can lag by about 10 seconds.</li><li>If you decline the prompt DataRework still works, but the totals will not update until the service is installed. The prompt appears again the next time you start the program.</li></ul>
+
+<h3>Who can do what</h3>
+<p>What you can change depends on the <b>role</b> of your account. Your role is shown next to your name at the top right of the window.</p>
+<table class=""><thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Can do</th><th>Cannot do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>admin</b> / <b>super admin</b><br><small></small></td><td>Everything: search, browse, edit cells, delete rows, Save, rename containers, delete containers, report damage. <i>super admin</i> has exactly the same power as <i>admin</i>.</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>any other role (for example <b>user</b>)</td><td>Search, filter, browse containers, view records, <b>Report damage</b>.</td><td>Edit cells, delete rows, Save, rename or delete containers. These buttons are greyed out and show a tooltip such as “Only admins can save changes”.</td></tr></tbody></table>
+<div class="callout warn"><div class="t">Report damage is open to every role</div><p>The Report damage tab has no administrator check. Anyone who can sign in can flag a serial as damaged, and the app has no undo for it.</p></div>
+
+<h3>Barcode scanners</h3>
+<p>Keyboard-style scanners type the code and then press <kbd>Enter</kbd>. DataRework is built for this: in the <b>Value</b> box <kbd>Enter</kbd> runs the search; in the Rename and Delete dialogs <kbd>Enter</kbd> only <i>looks the serial up</i> and never confirms anything; in <b>Report damage</b> <kbd>Enter</kbd> submits the report straight away.</p>
+
+<h3>Remembered settings</h3>
+<p>DataRework remembers, per Windows user: the language (EN / TH), the colour theme (Dark / Light), and whether the filter panel is folded on each tab.</p>
+</section><section class="chapter pb" id="login"><h2><span class="num">3.</span>Signing in</h2>
+<figure class="narrow"><div class="frame"><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="The sign-in window."></div><figcaption>The sign-in window.</figcaption></figure>
+<ol class="steps"><li>Open <code>DataRework.exe</code>. The sign-in window appears.</li><li>Type your <span class="ui">Username</span> and <span class="ui">Password</span>. Tick <span class="ui">Show password</span> to check what you typed.</li><li>Press <kbd>Enter</kbd> or click <span class="btn blue">Login</span>.</li><li>The main window opens and the sign-in window closes.</li></ol>
+<p>You can switch the language with the <b>EN / TH</b> buttons at the top right of the sign-in window.</p>
+<h3>If sign-in fails</h3>
+<table class=""><thead><tr><th>Message</th><th>What it means and what to do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class="msg">Invalid username or password</span></td><td>The password is wrong. The password box is cleared; type it again.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="msg">This user does not have permission to Log in</span></td><td>Your account has been deactivated. Ask your administrator.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="msg">This user does not have permission to rework</span></td><td>Your account is not allowed to use this application. Ask your administrator to enable rework access.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="msg">Unable to reach the database</span></td><td>The computer cannot reach the company database (network, VPN or server problem). Check the connection and try again; if it persists, contact IT.</td></tr><tr><td>Nothing happens</td><td>If the username you typed does not exist the window may simply not respond. Re-check the spelling of the username.</td></tr></tbody></table>
+</section><section class="chapter pb" id="tour"><h2><span class="num">4.</span>A tour of the main window</h2>
+<figure class=""><div class="frame"><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="The main window on the Records tab. Numbers are explained below."><span class="mark" style="left:69.38%;top:2.37%">1</span><span class="mark" style="left:76.31%;top:2.37%">2</span><span class="mark" style="left:85.54%;top:2.37%">3</span><span class="mark" style="left:1.54%;top:9.47%">4</span><span class="mark" style="left:1.54%;top:30.66%">5</span><span class="mark" style="left:1.69%;top:37.63%">6</span><span class="mark" style="left:1.69%;top:46.05%">7</span><span class="mark" style="left:0.92%;top:92.37%">8</span><span class="mark" style="left:10.62%;top:92.37%">9</span><span class="mark" style="left:54.00%;top:92.37%">10</span><span class="mark" style="left:67.38%;top:92.37%">11</span><span class="mark" style="left:79.69%;top:92.37%">12</span><span class="mark" style="left:86.46%;top:92.37%">13</span><span class="mark" style="left:92.77%;top:92.37%">14</span></div><figcaption>The main window on the Records tab. Numbers are explained below.</figcaption></figure>
+<table class=""><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Part</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Language</td><td><b>EN / TH</b> switches the whole program between English and Thai instantly.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Theme</td><td><span class="ui">Dark</span> / <span class="ui">Light</span> colour theme.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Signed-in user</td><td>Your username and role.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td><span class="ui">Filters</span></td><td>Chooses which records to load. Applies to the Records and Container manager tabs.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Tabs</td><td><span class="tab">Records</span>, <span class="tab">Container manager</span> and <span class="tab">Report damage</span>.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td><span class="ui">Columns</span></td><td>Tick or untick to show or hide grid columns.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Record grid</td><td>One row per unit. Click a header to sort, double-click a cell to edit (admins).</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Status line</td><td>The latest result, in green (OK) or red (error).</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td><span class="btn light">Log</span></td><td>Opens the error log with full details.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td><span class="btn ">Rename container…</span></td><td>Rename a container (admins).</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td><span class="btn ">Delete container…</span></td><td>Delete a container with everything inside (admins).</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td><span class="btn light">Delete</span></td><td>Marks the selected rows for deletion. Visible on the Records tab only.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td><span class="btn green">Save</span></td><td>Writes pending edits and deletions to the database after you confirm (admins).</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td><span class="btn red">Cancel</span></td><td>Clears the filters and empties the grid, discarding unsaved edits.</td></tr></tbody></table>
+<h3>Language and theme</h3>
+<p>Both choices take effect immediately and are remembered the next time you open DataRework.</p>
+<div class="callout note"><div class="t">A few texts are always in English</div><p>The Report damage tab, the <b>← Previous</b> / <b>Next →</b> card pager and the “Loading…” text have no Thai translation yet, so they stay in English even when TH is selected.</p></div>
+<figure class=""><div class="frame"><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="What a non-admin sees: the Del column is gone and Save, Rename container and Delete container are greyed out."></div><figcaption>What a non-admin sees: the Del column is gone and Save, Rename container and Delete container are greyed out.</figcaption></figure>
+</section><section class="chapter pb" id="filters"><h2><span class="num">5.</span>Filters</h2>
+<p>The filter panel decides which records are loaded. It sits above the tabs and is shared by the <b>Records</b> and <b>Container manager</b> tabs. It is applied when you click <span class="btn blue">Search</span> (or press <kbd>Enter</kbd> in the Value box).</p>
+
+<h3>Date: Day, Month, Year</h3>
+<ul>
+<li><b>Month</b> and <b>Year</b> start on <b>today’s</b> month and year, so you see this month’s records first. The Year list runs from last year to four years ahead.</li>
+<li>Leave <b>Day</b> on its placeholder to include every day. The day list shortens to the days the chosen month really has (February never offers a 30th).</li>
+<li>Select the first entry (“Month” / “Year”) to drop that part of the date filter.</li>
+</ul>
+<div class="callout warn"><div class="t">“My records are missing” — check the date first</div><p>Because Month and Year default to today, records created in other months do not appear until you change them. This is the most common reason a search looks empty. The date is <b>not</b> reset by <span class="btn ">Clear</span>.</p></div>
+
+<h3>Product name and Ass. job no.</h3>
+<ul>
+<li>Type a value or pick one from the drop-down. A value you type that is not in the list is still used.</li>
+<li>The lists are <b>cascading</b>: they only offer values that exist for the chosen date, and the product list narrows further once an Ass. job no. is chosen. Each list shows up to 300 values.</li>
+<li>The match is exact (equal to what you typed).</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h3>Category, Tag and Value — find one specific serial</h3>
+<ol class="steps"><li>Pick a <span class="ui">Category</span>: Unit, Inner, Display or Carton.</li><li>Pick a <span class="ui">Tag</span>: Serial No, Roll No, Source ID or Target ID (only those that exist for that level are offered). <span class="ui">Serial No</span> is chosen for you.</li><li>Type or scan the <span class="ui">Value</span>, or pick one from the list (typing narrows the list).</li><li>Press <kbd>Enter</kbd> in the Value box, or click <span class="btn blue">Search</span>.</li></ol>
+<div class="callout note"><div class="t">Exact match</div><p>The value must match <b>exactly</b>, ignoring upper/lower case: searching for <code>1</code> finds <code>1</code> but not <code>10</code> or <code>201</code>. You may use <code>%</code> (any characters) and <code>_</code> (one character) as wildcards.</p></div>
+<p>If the filter is incomplete the search still runs, and the status line warns you, for example <span class="msg">category/tag filter skipped (no value entered)</span>.</p>
+
+<h3>Search, Clear and folding the panel</h3>
+<table class=""><thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class="btn blue">Search</span></td><td>Loads the Records grid using the filters. It also leaves any single-container view (see “Showing the records in …”).</td></tr><tr><td><span class="btn blue">Clear</span></td><td>Empties Ass. job no., Product name, Category, Tag and Value and returns the Container manager to “All cartons”. It does <b>not</b> reset the date and does <b>not</b> reload or empty the grid — rows already shown stay until you search again.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="btn light">Hide filters</span> / <span class="btn light">Show filters</span></td><td>Folds the panel to one line that still summarises what is filtered (for example “Filtering by October 2026 · AJ-26-0042”). The Records tab starts unfolded; the Container manager tab starts folded. Each tab remembers your choice.</td></tr></tbody></table>
+<figure class=""><div class="frame"><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="The filter panel folded away: the summary line still tells you what is filtered."></div><figcaption>The filter panel folded away: the summary line still tells you what is filtered.</figcaption></figure>
+</section><section class="chapter pb" id="records"><h2><span class="num">6.</span>The Records tab</h2>
+<h3>Loading records</h3>
+<p>Set your filters and click <span class="btn blue">Search</span>. The status line reports <span class="msg">24 row(s) loaded.</span></p>
+<div class="callout warn"><div class="t">The grid shows at most 500 rows</div><p>Rows come back in no particular order and the list stops at 500. If you see exactly 500, there are probably more: narrow the filters (day, job, a specific serial). Sorting a column only re-orders the rows already loaded; it does not fetch the “first” or “last” ones.</p></div>
+
+<h3>Reading the grid</h3>
+<ul>
+<li>Each row is one unit. The columns begin with the record <code>id</code>, the job, product and creation time, followed by four columns for each level: Serial No, Roll No, Source ID and Target ID.</li>
+<li><b>Hide columns</b> you do not need by unticking them in the <span class="ui">Columns</span> strip (scroll it sideways if it is long). Hiding only changes what you see.</li>
+<li><b>Sort</b> by clicking a column header: ▲ ascending, click again ▼ descending. Numbers sort as numbers. The delete-tick column cannot be sorted.</li>
+<li>Drag the edge of a header to change a column’s width. Click, drag, or Shift/Ctrl-click to select rows.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h3>Editing cells (admins)</h3>
+<ol class="steps"><li>Double-click a cell (or select it and press <kbd>F2</kbd>), type the new value, press <kbd>Enter</kbd>.</li><li>The cell turns <b style='background:#7fe0b0;color:#06301c;padding:0 5px;border-radius:3px'>green</b>: it is changed on screen but <b>not yet saved</b>. Typing the original value back removes the green.</li><li>Edit as many cells as you need, then click <span class="btn green">Save</span> (see below).</li></ol>
+<figure class=""><div class="frame"><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="A green cell (edited, unsaved) and a red row (marked for deletion)."></div><figcaption>A green cell (edited, unsaved) and a red row (marked for deletion).</figcaption></figure>
+<h4>Cells you cannot edit</h4>
+<p>The display, inner and carton <b>Serial No</b> and <b>Roll No</b> cells are read-only: one container label is shared by many rows, so changing it in one row would silently change it for the others. Hover over such a cell to read the explanation. To change a container serial, <b>right-click</b> the cell and choose <i>Rename this …</i> (see “Renaming a container”). Unit cells are editable because a unit belongs to exactly one row.</p>
+<div class="callout note"><div class="t">What changing a unit serial also does</div><ul><li>The <b>roll number</b> that belongs to the new serial is looked up in the serial inventory and filled in for you (unless you typed a roll number yourself in the same save).</li><li>The <b>old serial is freed</b> (no longer in use) and the <b>new serial is marked in use</b>.</li><li>The new serial <b>must exist in the serial inventory</b>. If it does not, the save is refused and nothing is changed.</li></ul></div>
+
+<h3>Deleting records (admins)</h3>
+<ol class="steps"><li>Tick the <span class="ui">Del</span> box at the start of each row you want to remove — or select rows (click, drag, Shift/Ctrl) and click <span class="btn light">Delete</span> in the footer, which ticks them all.</li><li>The rows turn <b style='background:#f28b8b;color:#3d0606;padding:0 5px;border-radius:3px'>red</b>. Nothing is deleted yet. Untick the box to undo.</li><li>Click <span class="btn green">Save</span> and confirm.</li></ol>
+<p>When rows are deleted their unit serials are <b>freed</b>, their links are removed, and any display or inner box that is left with nothing inside is removed too (its serial is freed as well). Other rows in the same box are never touched. To remove a whole box and everything in it, use <b>Delete container</b> instead.</p>
+
+<h3>Saving</h3>
+<ol class="steps"><li>Click <span class="btn green">Save</span>. If there is nothing to save the status line says <span class="msg">No changes to save.</span></li><li>A confirmation asks, for example: <i>“This will update 1 row(s) and delete 1 row(s) in public.filling_product_logs. This cannot be undone. Continue?”</i> The default answer is <b>No</b>.</li><li>Click <b>Yes</b>. The status line reports <span class="msg">Changes saved.</span> and the grid reloads with your changes.</li></ol>
+<p>Saving is <b>all or nothing</b>: every edit and deletion is written in one step, and if any part fails (for example a serial that is already in use) nothing is written at all. The error appears in red in the status line.</p>
+
+<h3>Cancel does not mean “undo”</h3>
+<div class="callout stop"><div class="t">Cancel throws everything away</div><p>The footer <span class="btn red">Cancel</span> button clears all the filters <b>and empties the grid</b>, discarding every unsaved edit and deletion mark — without asking. The status line says “Cleared.”. To undo a single edit, retype the original value; to undo a deletion mark, untick the box. To simply reload, use <span class="btn blue">Search</span>.</p></div>
+
+<h3>Right-click menu</h3>
+<p>Right-click a display, inner or carton <b>Serial No</b> cell (admins) to get <i>Rename this display…</i> / <i>Delete this display…</i> (the word follows the column). The dialog opens with that serial already filled in.</p>
+<p>Rename, Delete container, and opening a container’s records are <b>refused while you have unsaved edits</b>, because they reload the grid. Save or cancel first.</p>
+
+<h3>Showing one container’s records</h3>
+<p>When you click <span class="btn ">View records</span> on a container card, the grid shows only that container’s rows and a bar appears above it, such as “Showing the records in  ▢ C260800001 › ▢ I26080011 › ▢ D260800111”. Click <span class="btn light">Show all records</span> to return to whatever the filter panel says. Your filters are never changed by this.</p>
+<figure class=""><div class="frame"><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="The grid scoped to one display, with the way back in the bar above it."></div><figcaption>The grid scoped to one display, with the way back in the bar above it.</figcaption></figure>
+</section><section class="chapter pb" id="containers"><h2><span class="num">7.</span>The Container manager tab</h2>
+<p>This tab lets you browse the packing structure like folders: cartons contain inner boxes, inner boxes contain displays, displays contain units. Counts follow the active filters and include <b>all</b> matching saved rows (not limited to 500).</p>
+<figure class=""><div class="frame"><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="Top level: one card per carton."></div><figcaption>Top level: one card per carton.</figcaption></figure>
+
+<h3>Cards</h3>
+<p>Each card is one container. The coloured top edge and icon show its level: <b style='color:#d98a0b'>carton</b> amber, <b style='color:#7a52c9'>inner</b> violet, <b style='color:#2c6aa0'>display</b> blue, <b style='color:#2f9e66'>unit</b> green. A card shows the serial, how many items are inside, and how many saved rows it covers.</p>
+<table class=""><thead><tr><th>Button</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class="btn ">Open box</span></td><td>Goes <i>inside</i> this container and shows its children as cards.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="btn ">View records</span></td><td>Switches to the Records tab showing only this container’s rows. (On a unit card this is the main button.)</td></tr><tr><td><span style="color:#2c6aa0">Rename</span></td><td>Opens the Rename dialog for this serial (admins).</td></tr><tr><td><span style="color:#2c6aa0">Delete</span></td><td>Opens the Delete container dialog for this serial (admins). Unit cards have no Delete — units are deleted in the Records grid.</td></tr></tbody></table>
+<p>A card named <span class="ui">Unassigned</span> groups rows that have no container at that level. It has no serial, so it cannot be renamed or deleted.</p>
+<p>If a product picture has been set up, cards can show it as a small thumbnail (unit cards by default; box cards when an image is configured for that level); click it to enlarge. (Pictures are added by IT — see <code>docs/product-avatars.md</code>.)</p>
+
+<h3>Moving around</h3>
+<figure class=""><div class="frame"><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="Four levels deep: the trail “All cartons › carton › inner › display” sits above the unit cards."></div><figcaption>Four levels deep: the trail “All cartons › carton › inner › display” sits above the unit cards.</figcaption></figure>
+<ul>
+<li>The <b>trail</b> at the top (“All cartons › carton › inner …”) shows where you are. Click any part of it to jump back there.</li>
+<li><b>←</b> and <b>→</b> are Back and Forward through the places you visited (keyboard: <kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>←</kbd> / <kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>→</kbd>). Hover to see where they lead. The page you were on at each level is remembered.</li>
+<li>The box <i>Find serial in this level</i> filters the cards on the current level by part of a serial.</li>
+<li>At the bottom, <span class="btn light">← Previous</span> / <span class="btn light">Next →</span> turn pages (as many cards fit as your window allows, up to 20 per page).</li>
+<li><span class="btn ">Refresh</span> reloads the cards — use it after someone else has changed data.</li>
+<li>A strip such as “Filtering by AJ-26-0042” with <span class="btn light">Show all</span> explains why some containers are missing; <span class="ui">Show all</span> clears job, product, category, tag and value (the date stays).</li>
+</ul>
+</section><section class="chapter pb" id="rename"><h2><span class="num">8.</span>Renaming a container</h2>
+<p>Use this when a carton, inner or display label carries the wrong serial, or when you need to change a unit serial by scanning it. A rename is applied <b>immediately</b>; it does not wait for Save. Admins only.</p>
+<p>Start it from the footer button <span class="btn ">Rename container…</span>, the <span class="ui">Rename</span> link on a card, or by right-clicking a serial cell in the grid.</p>
+<figure class="narrow"><div class="frame"><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="Stage 1 — the Rename dialog."></div><figcaption>Stage 1 — the Rename dialog.</figcaption></figure>
+<h3>Stage 1: rename the container</h3>
+<ol class="steps"><li>Choose the <span class="ui">Level</span>: Carton, Inner, Display or Unit.</li><li>In the first box scan or type the <b>current</b> serial (or pick it from the list). Press <kbd>Enter</kbd> to jump to the second box. The line under the boxes tells you how much will change, e.g. <i>“12 row(s) and 15 link(s) name “I26080011”. All of them will be rewritten.”</i></li><li>In the second box scan or type the <b>new</b> serial. It starts filled with the old serial so you can edit just a few characters. A scanner’s <kbd>Enter</kbd> never submits the dialog.</li><li>Click <span class="btn blue">Rename</span> (enabled once the new serial differs from the old).</li><li>Read the confirmation (it states the number of rows and links) and click <b>Yes</b>.</li></ol>
+<p>The status line then reads, for example <span class="msg">Renamed inner "I26080011" to "I26089011" — 12 row(s), 15 link(s).</span></p>
+<p>The rename rewrites every row and link that names the serial, carries the roll number along, <b>frees the old serial and marks the new one in use</b>. It is refused (and nothing changes) if the new serial is already used somewhere, if the old one does not exist, or if the new serial is not in the serial inventory.</p>
+
+<h3>Stage 2: rename the children (after a carton, inner or display)</h3>
+<p>If the renamed carton, inner or display holds anything (inner boxes, displays or units), a second window, <b>Rename children</b>, lists what it holds. It is a separate step <i>after</i> the parent rename has already been saved.</p>
+<figure class="narrow"><div class="frame"><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="Stage 2 — carry the new naming down to the children you choose."></div><figcaption>Stage 2 — carry the new naming down to the children you choose.</figcaption></figure>
+<ul><li>Each row shows the old serial, a suggested new serial and how many items are inside. The suggestion replaces the old parent code found inside the child’s serial with the new one; if the child’s serial does not contain it, the suggestion is unchanged and nothing will be renamed until you type a new value.</li><li>Tick the rows to rename (use <span class="ui">Select all</span> / <span class="ui">Select none</span>), and edit any new serial by hand.</li><li>The button reads <span class="btn ">Apply</span> or “Apply N change(s)”. It stays disabled while two children would get the same serial (a warning says which).</li><li><span class="btn ">Skip</span> closes the window and leaves the children as they are. <b>The parent rename stays in place either way.</b></li></ul>
+<div class="callout warn"><div class="t">There is no Undo</div><p>To reverse a rename you must rename it back, which is a new rename subject to the same checks. Check the serials and counts in the confirmation before you click Yes.</p></div>
+</section><section class="chapter pb" id="delete"><h2><span class="num">9.</span>Deleting a container</h2>
+<p>Deleting a container removes the box <b>and everything inside it</b>: its saved rows, its links and everything below it. Admins only; it happens <b>immediately</b>, not at Save. Start it from <span class="btn ">Delete container…</span>, the <span class="ui">Delete</span> link on a card, or by right-clicking a serial cell.</p>
+<figure class="narrow"><div class="frame"><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="The Delete container dialog lists everything that will go before you confirm."></div><figcaption>The Delete container dialog lists everything that will go before you confirm.</figcaption></figure>
+<ol class="steps"><li>Choose the <span class="ui">Level</span>: Carton, Inner or Display (units are deleted in the Records grid).</li><li>Scan, type or pick the serial. <b>No container is pre-selected</b> and the Delete button stays disabled until a container has been found.</li><li>Read the list under <b>Inside this container</b> (nested items are indented) and the summary, e.g. <i>“12 unit(s) · 12 saved row(s) · 15 link(s) will be deleted.”</i> If a parent box would be left empty by this, a line says it will be removed too.</li><li>Click <span class="btn red">Delete</span>. <kbd>Enter</kbd> never confirms. A final question states the exact numbers; answer <b>Yes</b> to proceed.</li></ol>
+<p>On success <span class="msg">Deleted inner "I26080011" — 12 row(s), 15 link(s), 12 unit serial(s) released.</span> The serials of the container and everything under it are freed so they can be used again.</p>
+<h3>Safeguards</h3>
+<ul><li>If the contents changed between the moment you looked and the moment you confirmed (someone else is working on it), the delete is refused and <b>nothing is deleted</b>.</li><li>It refuses when the serial names more than one container, or when a container sits under more than one parent, rather than guessing.</li><li>Pending grid edits must be saved or cancelled first.</li></ul>
+<div class="callout stop"><div class="t">This cannot be undone</div><p>A deleted container, its rows and its links are gone. Always read the “Inside this container” list first.</p></div>
+</section><section class="chapter pb" id="damage"><h2><span class="num">10.</span>Report damage</h2>
+<p>Use this tab to flag a serial as <b>damaged</b> — for example a label that is destroyed and can never be scanned again. It works for a serial at any level: unit, display, inner or carton.</p>
+<figure class=""><div class="frame"><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="The Report damage tab."></div><figcaption>The Report damage tab.</figcaption></figure>
+<ol class="steps"><li>Open the <span class="tab">Report damage</span> tab. The cursor is already in the box.</li><li>Scan or type the damaged serial and press <kbd>Enter</kbd> (or click <span class="btn red">Report damage</span>).</li><li>On success the status below the box reads <span class="msg">Reported "U2608012345" damaged.</span> and the box is cleared, ready for the next scan.</li></ol>
+<h3>Only a serial that is not in use can be reported</h3>
+<p>If the serial is still attached to a unit or container in production, a window <b>Serial in use</b> asks whether to open it in the records editor.</p>
+<ol class="steps"><li>Click <b>Yes</b>. DataRework switches to the Records tab, filtered to that serial.</li><li>Take the serial out of use: delete the unit’s row, or delete the container, or rename the container onto a different serial (this frees the old one).</li><li>Return to the Report damage tab and report the serial again.</li></ol>
+<ul>
+<li>If the serial is not in the serial inventory at all, you see <span class="msg">No serial "X" found in staging_serial_data.</span> Check for a typing mistake.</li>
+<li>The damaged total for the roll updates within about 10 seconds (background service).</li>
+</ul>
+<div class="callout warn"><div class="t">Immediate, with no confirmation and no undo</div><p>Pressing <kbd>Enter</kbd> reports the serial at once. The app does not record who reported it or when, and offers no way to clear the flag. Double-check what you scanned. This tab does not delete or alter any records; it only flags the serial in the inventory.</p></div>
+</section><section class="chapter pb" id="status"><h2><span class="num">11.</span>Status line and error log</h2>
+<p>The line at the bottom left is the status line. <b style='color:#1f7a4d'>Green</b> means the last action worked; <b style='color:#b23b35'>red</b> means it did not. It shows one short sentence. Search warnings (for example a filter that was skipped) are appended after the row count.</p>
+<h3>The error log</h3>
+<p>Click <span class="btn light">Log</span> to open the <b>Error log</b> window. It lists the most recent errors with the time, the message and the technical detail, newest last. It stays open while you keep working.</p>
+<figure class="narrow"><div class="frame"><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="The error log window."></div><figcaption>The error log window.</figcaption></figure>
+<ul><li><span class="btn light">Copy</span> copies the whole log to the clipboard — paste it into your message when you ask IT for help.</li><li><span class="btn light">Clear</span> empties the log; <span class="btn light">Close</span> closes the window.</li><li>The log holds the last 500 entries in memory only. It is <b>never saved to disk</b> and is lost when you close DataRework, so copy it before you quit.</li></ul>
+</section><section class="chapter pb" id="recipes"><h2><span class="num">12.</span>Step-by-step recipes</h2>
+<h3>A. Find one unit by scanning it</h3>
+<ol class="steps"><li>Set Month/Year to when it was made (or leave today’s), set <span class="ui">Category</span> = Unit, <span class="ui">Tag</span> = Serial No.</li><li>Click into <span class="ui">Value</span>, scan the label. The scanner’s <kbd>Enter</kbd> runs the search.</li><li>One row appears. Scroll right (or tick columns in the strip) to see its display, inner and carton.</li></ol>
+<h3>B. Correct a wrongly scanned unit serial</h3>
+<ol class="steps"><li>Find the row (recipe A).</li><li>Double-click its Unit Serial No cell and type the correct serial. The cell turns green.</li><li>Click <span class="btn green">Save</span> → <b>Yes</b>. If the correct serial is not in the inventory, the save is refused with a message that nothing was changed.</li></ol>
+<h3>C. Take a unit out of its box</h3>
+<ol class="steps"><li>Find the row, tick its Del box, click Save → Yes.</li><li>The unit’s serial is freed. If that was the last unit in a display, the empty display is removed too.</li></ol>
+<h3>D. Fix a wrong carton, inner or display label</h3>
+<ol class="steps"><li>Click <span class="btn ">Rename container…</span> (or right-click the serial in the grid).</li><li>Pick the level, scan the current serial, scan the new one, <b>Rename</b>, <b>Yes</b>.</li><li>In the Rename children window tick the children that should follow, <b>Apply</b> — or <b>Skip</b>.</li></ol>
+<h3>E. Remove an entire box</h3>
+<ol class="steps"><li>Click <span class="btn ">Delete container…</span>, pick the level, scan the serial.</li><li>Read what is listed inside. Click Delete, then <b>Yes</b>.</li></ol>
+<h3>F. Report a damaged label</h3>
+<ol class="steps"><li>Open <span class="tab">Report damage</span>, scan the serial, <kbd>Enter</kbd>.</li><li>If told it is still in use, answer Yes, free it (recipe C or E, or rename), then report again.</li></ol>
+<h3>G. See what is inside a carton</h3>
+<ol class="steps"><li>Open <span class="tab">Container manager</span>, find the carton card (use the search box if needed), click <span class="btn ">Open box</span>.</li><li>Keep clicking <span class="btn ">Open box</span> to go down to displays and units, or <span class="btn ">View records</span> to see the rows in the grid.</li></ol>
+</section><section class="chapter pb" id="trouble"><h2><span class="num">13.</span>Troubleshooting</h2>
+<p>Messages appear in the status line (and in the error log).</p>
+<table class=""><thead><tr><th>Message</th><th>Meaning</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class="msg">Unable to reach the database</span></td><td>The computer cannot reach the company database.</td><td>Check the network/VPN, retry. Nothing was changed. Contact IT if it continues.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="msg">Table "public.…" was not found.</span></td><td>The records table is missing or misconfigured.</td><td>Tell IT; this is a configuration problem.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="msg">Only admins can save changes.</span></td><td>Your role is not admin / super admin.</td><td>Ask an administrator to make the change.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="msg">Save or cancel your pending edits before …</span></td><td>You have green/red unsaved rows and tried to rename, delete a container or open container records.</td><td>Click Save, or Cancel to discard, then retry.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="msg">Cannot save: display_serial_no "…" is shared with N other row(s). …</span></td><td>Your edit would change a container that other rows share.</td><td>Nothing was saved. Use Rename container instead.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="msg">That serial already exists for this assignment/parent: …</span></td><td>The new serial duplicates another one.</td><td>Nothing was saved. Use a different serial.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="msg">Serial "…" matches 0 row(s) in staging_serial_data; expected exactly 1. Usage totals would be wrong, so nothing was changed.</span></td><td>The new serial is not registered in the serial inventory (0 rows), or is registered twice.</td><td>Check the serial for typing mistakes. If it is correct, ask IT to add or fix its inventory entry.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="msg">Save failed: …</span></td><td>The database refused the save for another reason.</td><td>Nothing was saved. Open Log, Copy, and send it to IT.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="msg">Serial "…" is already in use — pick another.</span></td><td>Rename: the new serial is already on another row or link.</td><td>Choose a serial that is not used.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="msg">No carton named "…" exists in staging_product_logs.</span></td><td>Rename: the current serial does not exist at that level.</td><td>Check the level and the serial.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="msg">Rename failed: …</span></td><td>Another database problem during rename.</td><td>Check Log; the parent rename is all-or-nothing.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="msg">Children unchanged: …</span></td><td>The parent was renamed, but renaming the children failed.</td><td>Rename the children again with Rename container.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="msg">Two children would share …</span></td><td>Two rows in the Rename children window have the same new serial.</td><td>Edit one of them.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="msg">The contents of … changed since you looked … Nothing was deleted.</span></td><td>Someone changed the container while you had the Delete window open.</td><td>Open Delete container again and review the list.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="msg">… is linked under more than one parent … refusing to guess</span></td><td>The data shows the container under two parents.</td><td>Do not force it; ask IT to correct the data.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="msg">The database refused to delete … Nothing was deleted.</span></td><td>A database rule blocked the delete and it was rolled back.</td><td>Copy the log and send it to IT.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="msg">… is still in use, so it cannot be reported damaged yet.</span></td><td>The serial is active in production.</td><td>See “Report damage” — free it first.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="msg">Could not find "…" in the records -- search for it manually.</span></td><td>The app could not tell which level the serial belongs to.</td><td>Search for it yourself with Category/Tag/Value.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="msg">Unable to load containers. Try Refresh.</span></td><td>The Container manager could not load.</td><td>Click Refresh; check the connection.</td></tr><tr><td>Search shows no rows</td><td>Usually the date (Month/Year) or an old Value is still set.</td><td>Check Month/Year first, then the other filters; click Clear and search again.</td></tr><tr><td>Serial totals do not change</td><td>The background service is not running, or it has not cycled yet (~10 s).</td><td>Wait 10–20 s; if still wrong, restart DataRework and accept the UAC prompt, or ask IT.</td></tr></tbody></table>
+</section><section class="chapter pb" id="reference"><h2><span class="num">14.</span>Quick reference</h2>
+<h3>What each role can use</h3>
+<table class=""><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>admin / super admin</th><th>other roles</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Search, filters, sort, hide columns</td><td class="yes">✓</td><td class="yes">✓</td></tr><tr><td>Browse Container manager, View records</td><td class="yes">✓</td><td class="yes">✓</td></tr><tr><td>Report damage</td><td class="yes">✓</td><td class="yes">✓</td></tr><tr><td>Error log</td><td class="yes">✓</td><td class="yes">✓</td></tr><tr><td>Edit cells / mark rows for deletion / Save</td><td class="yes">✓</td><td class="no">✗</td></tr><tr><td>Rename container</td><td class="yes">✓</td><td class="no">✗</td></tr><tr><td>Delete container</td><td class="yes">✓</td><td class="no">✗</td></tr></tbody></table>
+<h3>Limits to remember</h3>
+<table class=""><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Rows in the Records grid</td><td>500, in no fixed order</td></tr><tr><td>Values offered in Job / Product lists</td><td>300 (you can still type any value)</td></tr><tr><td>Container cards per page</td><td>up to 20, fewer on a small window</td></tr><tr><td>Error log</td><td>last 500 entries, memory only</td></tr><tr><td>Serial totals refresh</td><td>about every 10 seconds</td></tr></tbody></table>
+<h3>Keys</h3>
